Package: missile-defense
Architecture: any
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends}
Recommends:
 libasound2t64 | libpulse0
Description: faithful Atari Missile Command clone (Qt 6 + Vulkan)
 A modern, faithful clone of Atari's Missile Command (1980), built on a
 deterministic C++ simulation and rendered with Qt 6 and Vulkan.
 .
 Defend six cities from descending waves of ICBMs, splitting MIRVs and
 blast-dodging smart bombs by launching interceptors from three ammo-limited
 batteries. It features procedurally generated retro sound effects and music,
 a persistent high-score table with arcade initials entry, an options screen,
 and fullscreen support.
 .
 The audio backend (ALSA or PulseAudio) is loaded at runtime if present; the
 game runs silently if neither is available, hence the Recommends.
 .
 This package is the game alone and depends on no Python. Training your own
 agent is the separate missile-defense-training package.

Package: python3-md
Section: python
Architecture: any
Depends:
 ${python3:Depends},
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
 python3-numpy
Description: Python bindings for the Missile Defense simulation
 The deterministic C++ Missile Command simulation from the missile-defense
 game, exposed to Python as a batched, vectorised environment for reinforcement
 learning, together with the evaluation protocol the scripted baseline is
 measured with.
 .
 This is the library half only: it needs neither the game nor a GUI toolkit, so
 it is usable on a headless machine. It does not depend on PyTorch — the
 trainer does, and says so when you run it without one.

Package: missile-defense-training
Architecture: all
Depends:
 ${python3:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
 python3-md (>= ${source:Version}),
 python3-pyside6.qtcharts,
 python3-pyside6.qtwidgets
Recommends:
 missile-defense
Suggests:
 python3-torch,
 python3-pynvml
Description: training console for the Missile Defense learned agent
 A desktop console for training, watching and comparing learned Missile Defense
 policies: it starts and stops runs, plots the score against the scripted
 baseline live, lists the episodes a run recorded, and opens them in the game.
 .
 The console never needs PyTorch itself — it starts the trainer as a separate
 process, so a run survives the console being closed or crashing, and it can
 install a managed training runtime for you if this machine has no PyTorch.
 That is why python3-torch is only Suggested.
 .
 Installing this pulls in PySide6 (LGPL-3), which is why it is a package of its
 own: the game is MIT and depends on none of it.
